To rotate a PDF in Delphi 7, you can use a third-party library or component that can handle PDF files. Here are a few options:

1. **PDFlib**: This is a popular PDF library for Delphi, but it's no longer actively maintained. You can still download the older versions and use them in your Delphi 7 project. [PDFlib for Delphi](https://www.pdflib.com/products/pdflib-delphi/)
2. **T_VARIABLES**: This is a Delphi component that can manipulate PDF files, including rotation. You can download the component and use it in your Delphi 7 project. [T_VARIABLES](https://code.google.com/archive/p/t-variables/)
3. **PDFCore**: This is another Delphi component that allows you to create, read, and manipulate PDF files. It includes support for rotating pages. [PDFCore](https://sourceforge.net/projects/pdfcore/)

Here's an example of how you can use the T_VARIABLES component to rotate a PDF in Delphi 7:
```delphi
uses
  Vcl.Forms, Vcl.Controls, Vcl.ImageList, Vcl.StdCtrls, Vcl.Grids, Vcl.Menus, Vcl.Dialogs,
  T VARIABLES;

type
  TForm1 = class(TForm)
    Button1: TButton;
    procedure Button1Click(Sender: TObject);
  private
    { Private declarations }
  public
    { Public declarations }
  end;

var
  Form1: TForm1;

implementation

{$R *.dfm}

procedure TForm1.Button1Click(Sender: TObject);
var
  PDF: TPDF;
  Page: TPage;
begin
  PDF := TPDF.Create;
  try
    PDF.Load('input.pdf'); // Load the PDF file
    Page := PDF.Pages[0]; // Get the first page
    Page.Rotate(90); // Rotate the page 90 degrees
    PDF.Save('output.pdf'); // Save the rotated PDF
  finally
    PDF.Free;
  end;
end;
```
In this example, we create a TPDF object and load the input PDF file using the `Load` method. We then get the first page of the PDF using the `Pages` property, rotate it 90 degrees using the `Rotate` method, and save the rotated PDF using the `Save`

